1. Take a root node, and recursively construct three full ternary trees with (n-1) vertices each (left, middle, and right subtrees).
2. Take a root node, and recursively construct a full ternary tree with (n-2) vertices and add two subtrees as children of the root node.
3. Take a root node, and recursively construct a full binary tree with (n-2) vertices and add one leave as a child of the root node.

So, the recurrence relation for the number of full ternary trees with n vertices, denoted as C_n, can be written as:

C(n) = 3 * C(n-1) + 2 * C(n-2) + C(n-3)

Here, C(n) represents the number of full ternary trees with n vertices.

